@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+# 0009 — Cities, warehouses and stock per warehouse from day one
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+**Context.** The business starts in Visakhapatnam but must grow to several cities without a rewrite.
+Stock was one number per product (`products.stock_qty`); orders and customers had no location.
+
+**Decision.** Migration 006 adds `cities`, `warehouses` (one default per city) and `service_zones`
+(one pin code → one warehouse). Every order, customer and stock movement carries `city_id` and
+`warehouse_id`. Stock is held in `warehouse_stock` per warehouse; `products.stock_qty` stays as the
+total so existing screens keep working. `place_order` reserves stock in the customer's warehouse,
+which is derived from their pin code at sign-up (or set by an admin). Existing data is backfilled into
+one city ("Visakhapatnam") and one default warehouse.
+
+**Alternatives considered.**
+- *Keep one stock number and add a city label only.* Cheapest, but a second warehouse would need the
+  rewrite this decision avoids.
+- *Map polygons for zones.* More precise than pin codes but needs a maps dependency; pin codes are what
+  customers know and what Hyperpure asks for. Polygons can be added later without changing the model.
+
+**Consequences.**
++ A new city is set-up work (city, warehouse, pin codes), not code.
++ Reports can filter by city; team members can be limited to a city or warehouse (ADR 0010).
+− Customers with an unserved pin code cannot be approved until an admin sets a warehouse.
+− Two stock figures (per warehouse and total) must stay in step; only `apply_stock_movement` may touch them.
diff --git a/docs/adr/0010-team-roles-permission-matrix-scope.md b/docs/adr/0010-team-roles-permission-matrix-scope.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c79ecdf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0010-team-roles-permission-matrix-scope.md
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# 0010 — Team roles, a permission matrix and city / warehouse scope
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+**Context.** `profiles.role = 'admin'` meant "can do everything". The owner wants to grant team members
+access per module and action, limited to the city or warehouse they work in, and to change those
+grants from a settings screen rather than in code.
+
+**Decision.** Keep `profiles.role` as the *app* a login uses (admin / restaurant / delivery). Add
+`profiles.team_role` (super_admin, city_manager, warehouse_manager, procurement_buyer, pricing_manager,
+finance, sales, support, dispatcher, picker, driver), the table `role_permissions(team_role, module,
+action)` and `profiles.city_id` / `warehouse_id` as scope. Two SQL helpers do the checks:
+`has_permission(module, action)` and `in_my_scope(city, warehouse)`. Every admin-app function and
+policy uses them instead of `app_role() = 'admin'`. `super_admin` needs no rows and is the only role
+that may change the matrix or grant super admin. The browser mirrors the checks only to hide buttons.
+
+**Alternatives considered.**
+- *More enum values in `user_role`.* Simple, but a role change would need a migration and could not be
+  limited per module.
+- *Row-level grants per person.* Flexible, but hard to explain and audit. Role × module × action is what
+  the owner asked for and what every ERP does.
+
+**Consequences.**
++ The owner can create a "finance only" or "Vizag warehouse only" login without a developer.
++ Permission tests prove each role sees only what it should (security_test T20–T29).
+− New features must name their module and action, and check it in SQL, or they are super-admin-only by accident.
+− A promoted customer (role → admin) defaults to super_admin; the Team screen should set the right role straight away.
diff --git a/docs/adr/0011-sectors-and-trading-terms.md b/docs/adr/0011-sectors-and-trading-terms.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..acd9ec5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0011-sectors-and-trading-terms.md
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# 0011 — Sectors (customer types) carry the trading terms
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+**Context.** Restaurants, hotels, institutions (hospitals, hostels), defence units and small retailers
+buy differently: minimum order, delivery fee, credit days, purchase-order numbers, approval before
+dispatch. Hard-coding "restaurant" rules into `place_order` would mean a code change per sector.
+
+**Decision.** A `sectors` table (code, name, min_order_value, delivery_fee_below_mov, credit_days,
+needs_po_reference, needs_order_approval, can_self_register) seeded with five sectors. A customer
+(`restaurants.sector_code`) inherits its sector's terms and may override MOV, fee and credit days.
+`place_order` reads the terms: below the minimum it charges the fee if there is one, else refuses; it
+demands a PO reference where the sector needs one; it snapshots the sector on the order. The cart shows
+the same rule through `my_order_terms()` and `orderPreview()` (same formula in SQL and JS, both tested).
+`needs_order_approval` is stored now and enforced in Phase 2 (approval before dispatch).
+
+**Consequences.**
++ Adding a sector is a row plus a line in the sign-up form, not a code change.
++ Terms are visible and editable by the owner (Settings → Sectors).
+− The table is still called `restaurants`; renaming it would touch every screen for no behaviour gain, so
+  the name stays and the table comment explains it.
